INT. REGATTA BOATHOUSE - MORNING

A long timber shed that smells of varnish and river. Eight-oared shells hang from racks like sleeping fish. Pale light slants through the slats.

COACH ELEANOR PRZYBYLSKI, 60s, windbreaker, whistle, sits on an overturned oar-box. Before her stand five rowers in damp unisuits: ZEBEDIAH "ZEB" HOLLINGSWORTH, 20s, stroke; MARISELA QUIROGA, 20s, bow; CASPIAN ADEBAYO, 19, coxswain, small and quiet; TOVE NIELSEN, 21, seat five; and BASHIR KARIMI, 23, who has not yet looked up.

She says nothing. The oars drip.

ELEANOR
You're late.

ZEB
Two minutes.

ELEANOR
Two minutes is a boat length.

MARISELA
Coach, it was fog.

ELEANOR
The fog was there when I got here.

Caspian shifts a megaphone in his hands. Marisela tightens a shoelace that does not need tightening. Tove studies a knot in the floorboards. Bashir finally lifts his eyes and meets hers.

She holds the look. Then she rises, crosses to the shell Number Four, runs her palm along its hull, kneels to check a rigger bolt, and then, without turning, tosses a wrench over her shoulder, which Zeb catches and Marisela passes to Caspian, who hands it to Tove, who hands it to Bashir, who looks at it as though it were a snake.

ELEANOR (CONT'D)
Seat seven's rigger is loose.

BASHIR
It was tight yesterday.

ELEANOR
Yesterday you were a different rower.

Bashir doesn't answer. He kneels, takes the wrench, and tightens the bolt with six slow turns.

CASPIAN
Coach, the forecast says headwind.

ELEANOR
Then we row into it.

ZEB
It's a hundred meters.

ELEANOR
It's two thousand, Zebediah.

Silence. The river laps outside.

ELEANOR (CONT'D)
Boat on shoulders.

They lift the shell as one. Eight hands, no words. They carry it out into the pale light, and Eleanor stays behind a moment, listening to their feet recede.
